"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][23]={"university_biology_en:M23-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M23-L1 – Ecological modelling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Advanced Ecology and Environmental Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L3<\/a>: dynamics and\nmodelling.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish process and\nobservation models, calculate state forecasts and evaluate predictive\naccuracy outside the fitting data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Ecological models abstract processes to test hypotheses about\npopulation dynamics, material flows or spatial distribution. A model\nthat reproduces historical data well need not reliably predict new\nobservations. Model structure should follow the research question and\nthe available observation scale.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"state-space-and-measurement-process\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State space and measurement\nprocess<\/h3>\n<p>A discrete state-space model separates a latent state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(population) from a measured quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA simple open-population model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{t+1}=sN_t+b+\\eta_t,\\qquad Y_t=qN_t+\\epsilon_t.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the expected fraction surviving and remaining in the area per time\nstep,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis mean recruitment per step, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an observation factor. The terms\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent process and measurement deviations respectively. The additive\nmodel is an approximation; integer-valued nonnegative distributions are\nmore plausible for small populations. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq s&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe deterministic fixed point is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N^*=b/(1-s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDeviations from it shrink by a factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper step.<\/p>\n<p>Parameters are not always identifiable. A small observed population\ncan reflect low abundance or low detectability. Repeated observations\nand independent calibration may help. Alternative model structures\ninclude density dependence, changing environmental conditions and age\nclasses.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"forecasting-and-model-evaluation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Forecasting and model\nevaluation<\/h3>\n<p>Forecasts distinguish process, parameter, observation and, where\nrelevant, scenario uncertainty. Time series benefit from time-ordered\nvalidation: train on earlier data and evaluate later forecasts. Random\nshuffling can obscure temporal dependence. A simple benchmark forecast\nis the last observed value. Root mean squared error (RMSE) is one\npossible metric for point forecasts; probabilistic forecasts also\nrequire calibration and appropriate interval widths. New observations\ncan then inform a documented model update.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_120_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Model abundance approaches the fixed point of 50 under constant parameters.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model abundance approaches the fixed\npoint of 50 under constant parameters.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Model abundance approaches the fixed point of 50 under\nconstant parameters.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a fictional wetland, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout random deviations,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1=90<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>82<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_2=82<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N^*=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExpected detections at the second time point are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>49.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">49.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas a mean, not 49.2 individual animals. For separate later observations\n52, 48, 45 and predictions 50, 50, 48,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.38<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RMSE=\\sqrt{(4+4+9)/3}=2.38<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe value is meaningful only relative to a benchmark and with\nuncertainty considered.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the fixed point for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0.75,b=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0=120<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing those parameters.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should a model not be selected solely from its fitting\ndata?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20/(1-0.75)=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>110<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.75\\cdot120+20=110<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Flexible models can reproduce random patterns in the fitting data;\nseparate temporal test data assess transferability.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All numbers are simulated. The fixed point assumes time-constant\nparameters. An observation factor of 0.6 is not an empirically\nestablished detection probability. Model structure can be\nmechanistically wrong despite good short-term forecasts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>State models connect biological processes with incomplete\nmeasurements. The next lesson examines spatial dependence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210x.13104\">White et\nal. (2019; online 2018) – Developing an automated iterative near-term\nforecasting system for an ecological study<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forecast construction,\nevaluation and iterative updating; indexed text · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M23-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M23-L2 – Spatial statistics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Advanced Ecology and Environmental Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L4<\/a>: inference and\nspatial sampling.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Quantify spatial\nautocorrelation, justify spatial weights and plan spatially separated\nvalidation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Nearby sampling sites may share similar soil, climate and species\ncomposition. The assumption of independent residuals is therefore often\nproblematic in ecological data. Spatial dependence can represent either\na biological process or an artefact of sampling design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"morans-index\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Moran’s index<\/h3>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i=x_i-\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the centred measurement at site\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na predefined neighbourhood weight (zero diagonal). Global Moran’s index\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\frac{n}{\\sum_{ij}w_{ij}}\\frac{\\sum_{ij}w_{ij}z_i z_j}{\\sum_i z_i^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The definition requires nonconstant data and positive total weight.\nWith usual weights, positive values indicate similarity between\nneighbours and negative values spatial contrast. The null expectation\nunder random permutation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/(n-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot exactly zero. The possible bounds depend on the weight matrix. A\npermutation test assumes exchangeability under the null hypothesis;\nstructured sampling may require restricted permutation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"models-and-validation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Models and validation<\/h3>\n<p>Spatial models can account explicitly for residual correlation, for\nexample through a spatial covariance function or autoregressive error\nstructure. Geostatistical methods use the increase of differences with\ndistance, for example in a semivariogram. Choice of scale and\nneighbourhood influences results. For transfer to new regions, withhold\ncontiguous spatial blocks sized to match the dependence scale and\nintended use. Examine the spatial structure of residuals, not just raw\ndata. Spatial association does not establish a causal process.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_121_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four sites in two arrangements illustrate similar and alternating neighbouring values.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four sites in two arrangements illustrate\nsimilar and alternating neighbouring values.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Four sites in two arrangements illustrate similar and\nalternating neighbouring values.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Four equally spaced sites on a line have model values 2, 2, 6, 6.\nWeights are one for immediate neighbours in both directions and zero\notherwise. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(-2,-2,2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntotal weight 6, squared sum 16 and weighted product sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(4-4+4)=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=(4/6)(8/16)=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe null expectation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly four sites are insufficient for a robust spatial conclusion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the arrangement 2, 6, 2, 6 with the same weights.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is randomly splitting densely sampled spatial points into\ntraining and test sets risky?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does a directional river network change the modelling\nproblem?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Product sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsquared sum 16;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>24<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=(4/6)(-24/16)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Nearby test points may resemble training points closely; measured\naccuracy may overestimate performance in new regions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Flow direction and distance along the river network may be more\ninformative than symmetric Euclidean neighbourhoods.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The line example uses artificial data. Coordinate projection, grid\nresolution, boundaries and measurement error influence spatial analysis.\nReport alternative neighbourhood definitions as a sensitivity\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Spatial dependence is part of both modelling and validation. The next\nlesson connects spatial distribution with climate change.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://eref.uni-bayreuth.de/id/eprint/12970/\">Dormann et\nal. (2007) – Methods to account for spatial autocorrelation in the\nanalysis of species distributional data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Spatial dependence and\nregression methods; abstract · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M23-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M23-L3 – Climate change ecology<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Advanced Ecology and Environmental Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L2<\/a>: environmental\nchange, stress and space.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model climate-related range\nshifts, calculate climate velocity and distinguish exposure from\nbiological response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Climate change alters temperature, water availability, disturbance\nregimes and seasonal processes. Species respond through changes in\nphenology, demography, distribution or adaptation. The IPCC documents\nobserved changes and future risks to terrestrial and freshwater\necosystems. These differ by region, species and scenario.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"climate-velocity-and-ecological-niche\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Climate velocity and\necological niche<\/h3>\n<p>Under simplified conditions, the speed of a moving isotherm can be\nestimated from temporal temperature change and the magnitude of the\nspatial temperature gradient:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\frac{|\\partial T/\\partial t|}{|\\nabla T|}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With gradients in °C/km and trends in °C/year, the result is in\nkm/year. The formula describes movement of a climatic condition, not\nactual movement of a species. The ratio becomes unstable in regions with\nalmost no gradient. Topography and local microclimates modify exposure.\nSpecies distribution models relate occurrences to environmental\nvariables; projections into future climates require tested assumptions\nabout niche stability, dispersal and biotic interactions. Realised\nniches do not necessarily represent physiological tolerance limits.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"risk-and-uncertainty\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Risk and uncertainty<\/h3>\n<p>Exposure is contact with changed conditions, sensitivity is the\nstrength of biological responses, and adaptive capacity is the ability\nto limit negative consequences. Species may fail to reach suitable new\nclimates because of fragmentation or slow dispersal. Conversely,\nmicrorefugia can sustain local populations. Attribution studies compare\nalternative explanations for observed changes; projections explore\nconditional future pathways. Climate models, emissions scenarios and\necological model structures contribute different uncertainties. Ensemble\naverages must not be interpreted as a single certain future.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_122_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two model rates of 0.5 and 0.2 km per year produce a gap of 6 km after 20 years.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two model rates of 0.5 and 0.2 km per\nyear produce a gap of 6 km after 20 years.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two model rates of 0.5 and 0.2 km per year produce a gap\nof 6 km after 20 years.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional regional trend is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.03<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.03\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C/year}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith a gradient of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.06<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.06\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C/km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe isotherm moves at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkm/year in the appropriate direction. A model organism can colonise new\nsites at an average of only 0.2 km/year under the specified assumptions.\nOver 20 years these rates correspond to 10 km and 4 km respectively. The\ndifference of 6 km is a simplified spatial gap, not a predicted\nextinction probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate climate velocity for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.02\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C/year}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C/km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What property of a landscape corridor affects actual\ncolonisation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an observed range shift insufficient for causal\nattribution?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.02/0.1=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkm/year.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Connectivity, habitat quality and barriers determine whether\nindividuals can reach suitable sites and establish there.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Land use, sampling intensity, biotic interactions and other\nenvironmental changes must be tested as alternative explanations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All numbers are model data, not IPCC projections. The ratio ignores\nnonlinear and multidimensional climatic changes; species responses also\ndepend on life history and disturbances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Climate risks are mediated by space and biology. Conservation\nplanning can explicitly account for these differing conditions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg2/chapter/chapter-2/\">IPCC\n(2022) – AR6 WGII, Chapter 2: Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems and\nTheir Services<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections 2.4 and 2.5: observed responses and future\nrisks; indexed chapter text · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M23-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M23-L4 – Conservation planning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Advanced Ecology and Environmental Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L3<\/a>: biodiversity\nindicators and climate risks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Translate conservation targets\ninto a simplified optimisation model and assess complementarity, costs\nand trade-offs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Protected areas are not selected solely by the number of species\npresent. A network of sites should represent different conservation\nfeatures, support their long-term persistence and account for practical\nand social conditions. Complementarity describes a site’s additional\ncontribution to targets already achieved.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"a-target-coverage-model\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A target-coverage model<\/h3>\n<p>For planning units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand conservation features\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the amount of the feature in unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nits cost and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe selection decision. A simple model minimises<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i c_i x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>subject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i a_{ij}x_i\\geq T_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor every feature, where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the defined target. Costs can represent finance, management effort or\nforegone alternative use, provided the units are consistent.\nIncompatible targets may make the problem infeasible. A model with a\nbudget ceiling and maximised target achievement formulates a different\ndecision.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"from-map-to-decision\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From map to decision<\/h3>\n<p>A representative site network is not automatically ecologically\nviable. Connectivity, minimum area, temporal stability and habitat\nquality are important additional criteria. Uncertain populations may\nwarrant robust scenarios or safety margins. Results depend on the stated\ntargets and data; different weights produce different possible\nsolutions. Planning models support transparent decisions but do not\nreplace local rights, participation processes and legal review.\nSubsequent evaluation tests whether anticipated effects actually\noccur.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_123_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three sites with complementary features and costs; sites 1 and 2 are the cheapest complete pair.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three sites with complementary features\nand costs; sites 1 and 2 are the cheapest complete pair.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three sites with complementary features and costs; sites\n1 and 2 are the cheapest complete pair.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Three fictional sites contain conservation features A, B, C: site 1\ncontains A and B and costs 4 units; site 2 contains A and C and costs 3;\nsite 3 contains B and C and costs 5. Every feature must be represented\nat least once. All pairs cover the three features; their costs are 7, 9\nand 8. The cheapest pair is 1+2 for 7. Site 3 should not be preferred\nmerely because it adds a different feature: marginal contribution\ndepends on the existing selection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which combination is feasible and cheapest if site 2 is\nunavailable?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the original target feasible with a budget of 6?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two criteria that could argue against the cheapest pair despite\nfull target coverage.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sites 1 and 3, cost 9.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; every feasible pair costs at least 7 and no single site covers\nall features.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, lack of functional connectivity or low long-term\nhabitat quality; land-use rights must also be assessed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The example contains artificial binary occurrence data and additive\ncosts. Real populations require more than one detection per conservation\nfeature. Sensitivity to targets, data errors and future scenarios must\nbe disclosed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Systematic planning makes selection rules explicit. The next lesson\nexamines how appropriate field studies can evaluate conservation\neffects.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/35012251\">Margules &amp;\nPressey (2000) – Systematic conservation planning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Complementarity\nand systematic planning; abstract and figure descriptions · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M23-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M23-L5 – Monitoring and field research design<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Advanced Ecology and Environmental Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L4<\/a>: field sampling and\ncontrol groups.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain BACI design, calculate a\ndifference in differences, and assess causal interpretation and\nreplication limits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Ecological monitoring describes conditions and changes. An impact\nstudy also asks whether a particular intervention caused the observed\nchange. Natural temporal fluctuations and different starting conditions\nmake this distinction difficult.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"beforeaftercontrolimpact\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Before–After–Control–Impact<\/h3>\n<p>A BACI design measures an impacted group I and an unaffected\ncomparison group C before and after an event. A simplified interaction\nestimate is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>δ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat\\delta=(\\bar Y_{I,after}-\\bar Y_{I,before})-(\\bar Y_{C,after}-\\bar Y_{C,before}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This subtracts a shared temporal change from the change in the\nimpacted group. Causal interpretation assumes, among other things, that\nboth groups would on average have followed comparable trends without the\nintervention and that the comparison group is not affected by it.\nMultiple pre-intervention periods can provide evidence of differing\ntrends but do not guarantee this assumption. A single control site may\nbe atypical. Multiple independent sites and repeated time points help\ncapture spatial and temporal variation. Repeated measurements at the\nsame site are not independent intervention replicates.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"analysis-and-monitoring-strategy\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Analysis and monitoring\nstrategy<\/h3>\n<p>A linear model with group, time and interaction terms yields the BACI\ncontrast with appropriate coding; hierarchical models account for nested\nsites and temporal dependence. Count data may require other\ndistributions. Imperfect detection must not be mistaken for population\nchange; consistent survey methods and repeated visits help. Define the\noutcome, meaningful effect size, survey duration, independent units and\ndata quality rules in advance. Power analysis should match the intended\nmodel structure. Report results with intervals, raw-data summaries,\ndocumented missing observations and alternative explanations. Protection\nof sensitive species locations and required permissions are part of\nfield planning.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_124_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Model values 12 to 20 in the intervention group and 14 to 17 in the control group yield a contrast of 5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model values 12 to 20 in the intervention\ngroup and 14 to 17 in the control group yield a contrast of\n5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Model values 12 to 20 in the intervention group and 14 to\n17 in the control group yield a contrast of 5.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional mean species counts in a restoration project: impacted\nbefore 12, after 20; control before 14, after 17. The BACI contrast is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>17<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(20-12)-(17-14)=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspecies. The before–after difference alone would be 8 species. The\ncontrast of 5 is not robust evidence of effectiveness without\nreplication and an uncertainty interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the BACI contrast for I before 30, after 32, C before 28,\nafter 34.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are ten quadrats within a single restored site not ten\nindependent intervention replicates?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which condition is violated if the intervention also affects the\ncontrol site?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>34<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>28<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(32-30)-(34-28)=-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The intervention acts at site level; the quadrats share the same\nintervention and site conditions. They are subsamples.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The control no longer represents the trajectory without\nintervention; interference biases causal interpretation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The data are simulated. Multiple controls do not exclude unobserved\nconfounders. BACI is a design principle, not a guarantee of causality.\nModel choice, missing data, detection error and effect uncertainty\nrequire separate evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Advanced ecology connects forecasts, spatial analysis and testable\ndecisions. This elective prepares learners for independent environmental\nresearch.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://groups.nceas.ucsb.edu/monitoring-kb/resources/references/underwood-1994.html\">Underwood\n(1994) – On beyond BACI: sampling designs that might reliably detect\nenvironmental disturbances<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Limitations of single-control designs;\ninstitutional reference summary · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
